## Class Task — surface for Task.spawn / Task.yield_now / Task.join.
##
## `Task.spawn_raw(future_ptr: Int) -> Int` enqueues a Ruxen future
## (the i64 heap pointer of a class that `include Future dispatch
## runtime`) and returns the C-side TaskHandle pointer as i64. The
## helper does NOT poll the future — that happens later, during the
## pump call the block_on inline loop emits between iterations.
##
## v1 type laxness: callers pass a future-typed value where `Int` is
## declared. Ruxen's existing FFI codegen treats both at i64 (same
## pattern as `Thread.spawn_raw(closure: Int)` — the closure value
## flows in as a pointer). Type-safety improvement (typed
## `Task.spawn(fut: &var Future) -> TaskHandle[T]`) lands later once
## lib-decl grammar can express dyn-mixin params.
##
## `Task.yield_now -> TaskYieldFuture` is a static constructor.
##
## `Task.join(h: Int) -> TaskJoinFuture` is a static constructor —
## NOT a lib decl — that builds the join future for a spawned task.
##
## v1 user surface: `Task.join(h)` + hand-poll (see e2e fixture
## 728_async_spawn_join_basic). The `Task.join(h).await` sugar
## desugars syntactically (commit 8bab0e2 wired the
## class-static-method awaitee shape) but cannot RESOLVE
## TaskJoinFuture today because async_lowering's
## `collect_class_static_returns` / `collect_future_outputs`
## walk only the user-program AST, NOT bootstrap-loaded stdlib
## classes. Same gap defers
## tests/release-e2e/cases/731_class_static_call_await.rx.deferred
## (Async.sleep().await). When that architectural fix lands —
## making the desugar see bootstrap classes — this future becomes
## invisibly more ergonomic with zero stdlib changes.
class Task
  def self.yield_now -> TaskYieldFuture { TaskYieldFuture.new }

  def self.join(h: Int) -> TaskJoinFuture { TaskJoinFuture.new(h) }

  lib "runtime/scheduler.c"
    def self.spawn_raw as "ruxen_executor_spawn"(future_ptr: Int) -> Int
  end
end
